<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Portsmouth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Portsmouth ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>. It was signed on 5 September 1905 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>, after talks mediated by US President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s paramount interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, transferred its lease of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> and the railway south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, and ceded southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, but Japan received no indemnity, which provoked riots in Tokyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth.jpg" alt="Treaty of Portsmouth" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Russia_Treaty_of_Peace_5_September_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904 to 1905 pitted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, with one of the largest armies in the world, against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> only recently industrialised. Russian armies had been driven from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tsushima">Battle of Tsushima</a> was a disaster for the Russian navy. The war was unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, whose government faced growing threat of revolution, while Japan's economy was strained, its foreign debts were mounting and its supply lines in Manchuria were lengthening. As early as July 1904 the Japanese government sought intermediaries.</p><p>It approached US President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>, who had been pro-Japanese at first but later worried about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s growing power. Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> was at first adamant that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> would win. After the costly Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> the Japanese Foreign Minister, Komura Jutarō, judged that a settlement was critical. Russia replied favourably only after Tsushima, on 8 June 1905. Roosevelt chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>, partly for its cooler summer climate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The delegations arrived on 8 August. Komura led the Japanese side and the former finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-witte">Sergei Witte</a> the Russian. They stayed at a hotel in New Castle and were ferried each day to the naval base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittery-maine">Kittery, Maine</a>. Tsar Nicholas had forbidden any territorial concessions or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. Twelve sessions were held between 9 and 30 August. In the first eight the delegates agreed on a ceasefire, recognition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, Russian withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the transfer to Japan of the leases in southern Manchuria and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway">South Manchuria Railway</a>. Russia kept the Chinese Eastern Railway.</p><p>The last sessions dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and territory. Witte offered that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a> and drop its claim to reparations, warned that the war would resume, and had his delegation pack its bags. He pressed his case through the American media. Outmanoeuvred, Komura yielded: in exchange for the southern half of Sakhalin, Japan dropped its claim to reparations. The treaty was signed on 5 September, and ratified in Japan on 10 October and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 14 October 1905.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty brought three decades of peace between the two nations and confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as the leading power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Its second article acknowledged Japan's paramount interests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and Korea was annexed in 1910. The Japanese public, unaware how far its country's power was overextended, received the terms badly, and the frustration led to the Hibiya riots and the fall of the First Katsura Cabinet on 7 January 1906. Roosevelt received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1906, though he never went to Portsmouth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Portsmouth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Russo-Japanese War Japan took Russia's place: the <b>Treaty of Portsmouth</b> gave the Kwantung Army the right to occupy southern Manchuria, and the South Manchurian Railway, founded in 1906, dominated the economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Portsmouth</b> that ended the Russo-Japanese War in 1905 gave Japan Sakhalin south of the 50th parallel.</blockquote><a href="/state/karafuto-prefecture">Karafuto Prefecture</a></li><li><blockquote>By the <b>Treaty of Portsmouth</b> in 1905 Japan took over Russia's lease of Kwantung Oblast, including Port Arthur (Ryojun) and Dalny (Dairen), together with the railway south of Changchun.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Portsmouth</b> transferred the lease to Japan, which renamed it the Kwantung Leased Territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-oblast">Kwantung Oblast</a></li></ul></section>
